      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Jaegeuk, \n\nThanks for the great architectural suggestion! Using a workqueue to decouple the reclaim context and preserve __GFP_NOFAIL is indeed a classic and robust kernel pattern.\n  \nHowever, we are a bit concerned that this might introduce substantial overhead and complexity for Android/Mobile workloads in several aspects:\n  \n1. Adding a work_struct to f2fs_inode_info would increase the Slab size of every single inode. For Android devices with hundreds of thousands of cached inodes, this introduces a non-negligible permanent memory penalty. If we dynamically allocating the work items at eviction time would just move the memory allocation failure/deadlock to the workqueue itself, defeating the purpose of the workqueue.\n  \n2. Making f2fs_iget() wait for the workqueue could introduce synchronization bottlenecks or latency spikes right in the most critical fast-path, especially under heavy memory pressure when the workqueue gets backed up.\n  \n3. Implementing the workqueue, handling pending queues, and adding wait logic to f2fs_iget() requires 100+ lines of intrusive changes and introduces new race windows.\n  \nIn contrast, our current approach (which incorporates Chao Yu\u0027s suggestions) is extremely lightweight (~35 insertions). It leverages the existing F2FS checkpoint state machine (SBI_FSYNC_FLUSH_NODE) with absolutely zero memory overhead and zero locking overhead on f2fs_iget(). Allocation failures during direct reclaim are gracefully handled, which aligns perfectly with other fail-graceful paths in F2FS.\n\nGiven the strict memory and latency constraints of Android devices, we feel the Fail-Graceful approach is much more concise and optimal. What do you think?",
